Choice Benefit Cost or risk one coarse lock simple invariant and ordering unrelated operations contend striped locks parallelism across keys cross-stripe operations need ordering per-entry lock high concurrency lifecycle, memory, and lock-map races read-write lock parallel readers overhead and writer starvation policy optimistic read reader avoids acquisition validation and fallback complexity <p>For multiple locks, define one total order and use it everywhere. Account transfers can order by immutable account ID. Equal IDs require one lock, not locking the same non-reentrant mechanism twice.</p> <pre><code>static void transfer(AccountLocking from, AccountLocking to, long amount) {
    AccountLocking first = from.id() &lt; to.id() ? from : to;
    AccountLocking second = from.id() &lt; to.id() ? to : from;
    first.lock();
    second.lock();
    try {
        from.debitUnderLock(amount);
        to.creditUnderLock(amount);
    } finally {
        second.unlock();
        first.unlock();
    }
}
</code></pre> <p>The cross-account invariant is conservation: total balance is unchanged by a transfer. The linearization point is the completed debit-and-credit transition while both locks are held. In production code, hide <code>lock</code>, <code>debitUnderLock</code>, and <code>creditUnderLock</code> behind a package-private coordinator so callers cannot violate the protocol.</p> <p>This sketch requires distinct immutable IDs for distinct account objects, positive amounts, sufficient funds, and destination overflow validation before either mutation. Both mutations must be non-throwing after validation; locks do not roll back a debit when credit throws. Readers requiring a pair snapshot must acquire both locks in the same order, not call two individually locked getters. A self-transfer must be handled before acquiring two locks.</p> <p>Avoid these encapsulation leaks:</p> <ul> <li>returning the mutable collection guarded by the lock;</li> <li>using an interned string, boxed primitive, class object, or caller-provided object as a monitor;</li> <li>exposing methods that require callers to remember a lock;</li> <li>calling listeners, remote services, or overridable methods while locked;</li> <li>changing which lock guards a field across methods.</li> </ul>
